 **The unattended supervisor.** A `$vsss` run is meant to outlive its own quota window, and Codex's CLI has no durable loop of its own, so the image ships one: `/usr/local/bin/codex-supervisor` (root-owned, like the guard chain). It speaks Codex's app-server protocol on stdio — newline-delimited JSON, not JSON-RPC 2.0 — starting ONE thread with `approvalPolicy: "never"` and `sandbox: "danger-full-access"` (the only mode that works here; the container has no user namespaces for Codex's own bubblewrap sandbox, and the real boundary is the container, firewall and hooks described above), then re-entering that same thread with the literal word `continue` until the run's exit report ends with `VSSS-EXIT: <reason>`. It runs the vendor binary as `codex app-server` with no `-c` flags and a four-variable environment, so the system requirements and managed hooks apply unaltered. The reason it uses the protocol rather than `codex exec` is that only the protocol carries `codexErrorInfo` and the rate-limit snapshot: a quota failure names itself and reports when the window resets, so the supervisor sleeps until then plus two minutes rather than guessing, backs off on transient failures, declines every approval request it is sent (nobody is there to answer), and writes its thread id and counters to `.vss/codex-supervisor.json` after every change — so a killed supervisor resumes the same thread instead of starting a new one. Ceilings on turns, resumes, quota waits, retries and wall time bound the whole thing, and `codex-supervisor status --state <file>` prints where a run got to. It is wired into a Codex-led launch by a later item; today it is shipped and tested offline against a stub app-server.
 
+#### Codex-led sessions (`vibe --agent codex`)
+
+`vibe --agent codex` launches the ordinary vibe container — same image, same firewall, same mounts, same single-repo PAT — but starts the Codex TUI instead of Claude Code. The choice can also be a per-project **untracked** `.vibe/agent` file containing one word (`claude` or `codex`), or `VIBE_AGENT` in `~/.vibe/config`; the flag beats the file, the file beats the config var, and the default is `claude`. A tracked, symlinked or malformed `.vibe/agent` is ignored with one `⚠` line and the next source is used — a committed one would let a pull request choose the lead runtime of every container that clones the repo.
+
+Three prerequisites, all of them the Codex login mount's existing gates: `~/.codex` must exist on the Mac (from `codex -c 'cli_auth_credentials_store="file"' login`), the project must carry the untracked `.vibe-allow-codex` marker, and the project must be listed in `~/.vibe/codex-allow` (`vibe codex allow`). Miss any of them and the launch is refused **before** Docker does anything, naming both switches — Codex without its login is not a degraded session, it is a session that cannot work.
+
+What is enforced once it starts: the launcher hands off to `/usr/local/bin/codex-entry`, a root-owned script that runs `codex-guard-liveness` first and refuses to start Codex at all unless the whole chain proves itself — every file root-owned and not group-writable, the requirements constraints active, every managed hook in its hardened form, four known-bad calls actually denied through the real adapter, a benign one still allowed, and the `codex --version` floor met. Only then does it `exec` the unmodified `codex` binary, with no model, approval or sandbox flag of its own: those belong to the root-owned policy in `/etc/codex`, which is the only place they can be audited. A refusal prints the failing check and exits; nothing is started.
+
+**Claude Code remains the default and the recommended lead.** Codex-led sessions are new, and one capability is deliberately missing from them: overnight auto-resume and the stall watchdog are Claude-only for now — the marker is written by the `/vsss` skill inside a Claude Code session and the relaunch it drives is `claude --continue`. A Codex launch says so in one line before it starts; for unattended Codex work, run `codex-supervisor` inside the session instead. Ending the session returns you to a plain `vibe` launch with Claude leading, with nothing to undo.
+
 See **MANUAL-TESTS Test 54** for the post-relaunch end-to-end checks, and **Test 55** for the Codex system policy and liveness checks.
 
